// Political Thriller: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== political-thriller.f01 | feature | real-time
title: The Camera They Forgot
genre: political-thriller + drama
tone: Fluorescent, sleepless and tight as wire
era: 1981 | period
setting: A parliament chamber and a state television building in a southern European capital | fictional | institution
logline: When soldiers storm a young democracy's parliament during a vote and order the television cameras switched off, one cameraman leaves his running with the red light covered, and a night-shift news editor has to get the pictures on air before the tanks decide which side they are on.
premise: The dictator has been dead six years. The constitution is three years old. On a winter evening a moustached colonel walks into the chamber with two hundred gendarmes and fires a submachine gun into the ceiling. Three hundred and fifty deputies go under their desks. Three do not. In the press gallery {pedro} tapes over a tally light.
synopsis: The film runs from early evening to the following noon. In the chamber: gunfire, plaster, an order to lie down. An old general who is deputy prime minister, {old_general}, walks towards the guns and is jostled and does not fall. The colonel, {the_colonel}, announces that a competent military authority will arrive shortly. Nobody arrives. The film's suspense is in that wait. At the television building across the city, soldiers occupy the lobby and tell the staff to broadcast marches. {maite}, a news editor on the late shift, is receiving on an internal monitor, by a line nobody thought to cut, a mute wide shot of parliament from a camera its captors believe is dead. She has, in effect, the coup, live, and no way to transmit it. Through the night there are three questions: which regional commanders will bring tanks out (one does, and his armour sits in the streets of a coastal city with engines running); what the young king, in his palace with a telephone, will say and when; and whether a tape can leave a building with soldiers on the door. {maite} and a sound man, {quique}, copy the feed onto cassettes. The officer in the lobby, {captain_at_tv}, a tired man who has had no orders for two hours, is asked very politely whether he is sure he is on the winning side. He withdraws his men at nine. A palace crew arrives without lights. At a quarter past one in the morning the king appears in uniform and says, in ninety seconds, that the crown will not tolerate it. In the chamber the colonel listens on a deputy's transistor radio. The tanks on the coast go home before dawn. At noon the deputies walk out between ranks of the men who held them. {pedro}'s tape is broadcast that afternoon: thirty-four minutes the conspirators did not know existed, including a general who would not lie down. {maite} goes home and sleeps for fourteen hours.
question: How much of a democracy is a set of people each deciding, alone, not to obey?
themes: the record as a weapon; legitimacy as a broadcast; small refusals
audience: Adults; viewers who like procedure under pressure
viewpoint: Parallel: the chamber through {pedro}'s lens, the night through {maite}'s control room
stakes: A three-year-old constitution, three hundred and fifty hostages, and a return to dictatorship
conflict: Unarmed technicians and a few upright politicians against armed men waiting for an authority that may or may not come
ending: The king speaks; the tanks withdraw; the deputies walk free at noon. The tape nobody knew was running is shown to the country.
experience: A night of held breath, bureaucratic courage, and the charge of seeing an image get out
look: Green baize and tricorn hats under television lights; a control room of monitors; one silent wide shot repeated
sound: A burst of automatic fire, then long quiet; telephones; a transistor radio held to an ear; marches abruptly cut off
m: expose | institution | safety-vs-freedom | triumph
@ maite | Maite Urrutia | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 36 | The pictures on air | She was a student under the old regime | Soldiers hold the transmitter | Brisk, sardonic, calm when it counts | From shift editor to the person who asks a captain the right question | Rapid, dry, newsroom shorthand | Cardigan, chain-smoked cigarettes, a stopwatch
@ pedro | Pedro | co-lead | ally | human | he/him | 44 | To keep rolling | It is the only thing he knows how to do | A gun is pointing at the gallery | Stolid, unheroic, stubborn | From obeying the order to lie down to disobeying the one that mattered | Almost silent | Headphones, a strip of black tape, a tripod
@ the_colonel | The Colonel | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 49 | To hand the country to his superiors | He believes it is rotting | His superiors are waiting to see | Theatrical, sincere, increasingly alone | From master of the chamber to a man listening to a radio | Barrack-square shouts, then mutters | Tricorn hat, a moustache, a pistol held aloft
@ old_general | The Old General | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 68 | Not to lie down | He served the dictator and chose the constitution | He is one old man among two hundred rifles | Frail, furious, upright | Unmoved; that is his whole action | Orders, in a cracked voice | A dark suit, medals he is not wearing
@ quique | Quique | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 25 | To help, and not be shot | He is the youngest in the building | He has a van | Nervy, quick, funny when scared | From dubbing tapes to walking one past a sentry | Jokes under his breath | Flares, a satchel of cassettes
@ captain_at_tv | The Captain In The Lobby | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 33 | Orders | He was told an authority would call | The telephone has not rung for two hours | Correct, weary, thoughtful | From occupier to a man who marches his company out | Polite, formal | Field cap, a watch he keeps checking
~ maite - pedro | editor and cameraman | a feed she cannot acknowledge | a tape she puts on air
~ maite - captain_at_tv | editor and occupying officer | marches, by order | "are you sure you are on the winning side?"
~ the_colonel - old_general | mutineer and superior officer | an order to lie down | an old man still standing
