# The Wait | INT | Television Control Room | NIGHT | maite, quique | Receive it | Marches on the output. On an internal monitor, by a forgotten line: the chamber, live. She says nothing for a full minute. Then: get me cassettes | {maite} has the pictures | 6 | props=monitors+cassettes
# The Wait | INT | Television Lobby | NIGHT | maite, captain_at_tv | Ask him | A captain who has had no call in two hours. She brings him coffee and asks, as one professional to another, whether he is sure which side is winning | | 6 | crowd=30, props=rifles
# The Wait | INT | Parliament Chamber | NIGHT | the_colonel, pedro | Wait for the authority | The colonel promised a competent authority. The clock goes round. Deputies smoke under their desks. He telephones and is told to hold | Nobody comes | 5 | crowd=450
# 01:14 | INT | Television Control Room | NIGHT | maite, quique, captain_at_tv | Put the king on | The soldiers have gone. A palace tape, hand-carried. Ninety seconds in uniform. She counts it in with her stopwatch and takes the marches off | The king speaks | 7 | props=stopwatch+tape
# 01:14 | INT | Parliament Chamber | NIGHT | the_colonel, old_general | Hear it | A transistor radio passed along a bench. The colonel listens with his pistol hanging at his side. The old general looks at him | | 4 | crowd=450, props=transistor radio
# Noon | EXT | Parliament Steps | DAY | pedro, maite, quique | Walk out | Deputies between ranks of gendarmes. {pedro} carries his own tape down the steps. {quique} has the van running | The tape gets out | 5 | crowd=600, vehicles=broadcast van
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from an attempted coup in a parliament in 1981 and the camera left running, with invented people and an unnamed country
